     What they are made of is entirely theirs. Every one of the thirty-three reuses
     a "modelIds" array copied whole out of a record already in this dataset, with
     that record's own "modelRecolorFind" kept as-is and its "modelRecolorReplace"
     rotated so the copy is not simply its donor standing somewhere else. Nothing
     new was invented and no new cache id enters the data: the dataset still holds
     exactly 324 distinct model ids and 72 distinct animation names, the same two
     figures it held before this pass. Each record's animations are its donor's own
     or another animation on the same framemap as the body it wears, which is what
     kept the skeleton guard green.

     That sentence is the rule the whole pass rests on, and it is now enforced
     rather than promised: AuthoredRecordsTest.everyAuthoredKitIsAWholeCopyOfA
     ShippedKitWithThePaletteReDealt finds each record's donor in the shipped files
     and checks that the palette is a rotation of the donor's that actually changes
     it — so a hand-assembled kit, a reordered one, or a copy that forgot to re-deal
     is a red build. It was worth writing down because a review on 2026-08-29 made
     all three of those edits and the suite stayed green.

     Sixteen of the thirty-three had a body part added to their "modelIds" later the
     same day, along with thirty-one vendored records — see item 9. Each still
     matches its donor exactly, because the donor was repaired in the same way by
     the same rule, so the "copied whole" claim above still holds; what it now means
     is that the array is upstream's kit plus the part this project put back into
     it.

     One record was re-authored after that review. As first shipped, "Brother
     Alfric" (10290) copied the "Saradomin priest" (12854) — a donor carrying no
     recolour arrays at all, so there was nothing to rotate and the result was a
     visually identical copy of a citizen who walks Varrock's church. He was also
     one letter from "Brother Althric", a real NPC standing in that same monastery.
     He now ships as "Anselm" on the same uuid, wearing "Morten"'s (12853) kit with
     its five-pair palette re-dealt, and the exception this paragraph used to
     record no longer exists: all thirty-three are re-dealt.

     What is ours on these records is the writing and the placement: names, examine
     text, remarks, tiles, orientations and wander boxes. No upstream record was
     edited by this modification — the thirteen files gained records and lost
     nothing.

     None of the thirty-three tiles has been verified in game. Everything offline
     can say is that each figure stands within a few tiles of a placement upstream
     made, or inside a wander box upstream drew. docs/CITY-TOP-UP-CHECK.md is the
     list of what still has to be walked, and this is disclosed here because a
     licensee taking this data should know which placements carry a human's word
     and which carry ours.

  9. Forty-seven citizen records had a body part put back into their "modelIds" on
     2026-08-29 — thirty-one of them theirs and sixteen ours from item 8. Every one
     was shipping without part of a body: no trousers, no hands, or nothing on its
     feet. A human playing this plugin photographed two of them, and "who is this
     man, why does he not have any legs" is the complaint that got the predecessor
     plugin disabled by the hub, so this is a defect rather than a preference.

     Twenty of the forty-seven are one paste. Model 54275 is the Elder Chaos druid
     hood — two NPCs in the whole 1.12.36 cache wear it, and its geometry sits at
     head height — and twenty-six shipped citizens were carrying it, twenty of them
     at the array index where the kit they were copied from carries a trouser or a
     boot. "Mike" (12850) is the clearest: his kit is NPC 10758 "Gardener Jay Jr."
     with exactly one id changed, index 6, 28285 (legs) -> 54275. "Sefton" (12853)
     and "Corliss" (12338) carry it twice, at the two positions where legs and boots
     belong. Where the hood was standing in for a body part it was substituted back
     — 15 to 28285 (legs), 7 to 185 (boot). The remaining six records still carry it
     and are left alone: on those it is a spare hood rather than a missing leg, and
     removing it would take 54275 out of the dataset altogether.

     The other twenty-seven were repaired by adding the missing part. Twenty-six of
     those carried no hood at all; the twenty-seventh, "Alexander" (12853), carries
     one and still does — his was not standing in for anything, and what he was short
     of was a hand. Across all forty-seven the additions are 28 of 185 (boot), 9 of
     176 (hands) and 1 of 28285 (legs), landing in sixteen of the twenty-seven files.
     The whole repair, hood substitutions included, touched twenty-one files — an
     earlier version of this paragraph gave that larger figure for the additions.

     Every part used was already among the shipped model ids, which is why the
     dataset still holds exactly 324 distinct model ids and 72 distinct animation
     names — the same two figures item 8 records. Nothing else on any of the
     forty-seven records moved: tiles, orientations, wander boxes, names, examine
     text, remarks, recolours and animations are all untouched. The "npcAppearanceId"
     route used for "Rufus" at item 3 was deliberately not taken again; it would have
     changed both of those figures.

     src/test/java/com/matthewmariner/livelycities/BodySlotLintTest.java is what
     keeps this true, and src/test/java/com/matthewmariner/livelycities/BodySlots.java
     is the table it reads — which part of a body each of the 324 model ids is,
     measured once from the cache and transcribed, in the same way and for the same
     reason as ModelSkeletons.

     None of the forty-seven has been verified in game. Offline evidence is that each
     record now carries geometry at the shin, at the hand and on the floor; whether
     the part chosen looks right on that particular figure is a thing only somebody
     standing in front of it can say.

     This item is about geometry only. Having legs and having legs that are not
     painted the colour of a face are two different claims, and the second one is
     item 10.

